{"answer":"No supplied paper directly addresses “fixing systemic prejudices in generative suggestion engines by erasing the impact of problematic data points without full model updates.”

Closest matches are about debiasing or unlearning without costly full retraining, but they target recommender systems or language generation more broadly rather than generative suggestion engines specifically. FUDLR reframes debiasing for LLM-based recommender systems as efficient machine unlearning: it identifies bias-inducing samples to unlearn and then estimates/removes their influence on model parameters, aiming to avoid retraining while improving fairness and preserving accuracy [2]. CRAGRU also avoids full model retraining by decoupling retrieval and generation and reconstructing personalized recommendations via RAG-based generation to unlearn targeted user data while limiting collateral effects [1].

Differences: FUDLR targets bias-inducing samples and fairness in LLM recommenders [2], while CRAGRU targets targeted user-data unlearning in recommendation settings using RAG [1]. Other works discuss identity-robust generation via prompting-time neutralization, or side effects of preprocessing stereotype mitigation, rather than “erasing problematic data points” without updates [3,4].","grounded":false}
